`02-rubric/rubric.md` (about 420 lines) is written, before any clause extraction.

**Date check.** RFC 826 is November 1982, fourteen years and four months before RFC 2119. Verified against the pinned file: zero uppercase `MUST`/`SHOULD`/`MAY`/`SHALL`/`REQUIRED`/`RECOMMENDED`/`OPTIONAL`. The uppercase tokens that do appear (`REQUEST`, `REPLY`, `NOW` at 219, `AFTER` at 309) are opcode names and emphasis, and a keyword scanner would misread them. Appendix A is a full lowercase modal census with line numbers and a reproduction command. Two findings drove the design: only 2 of the 5 `must` occurrences bind an implementation of this protocol, and the reception algorithm at lines 197-225 contains exactly two modal words, both `optionally`. Every row therefore carries the requirement word actually present, or `(indicative)` / `(imperative)` / `(none: format line)`.

**Structure.** N1-N10 (behavior assigned to a party; the reception algorithm; format and encoding; wire-visible constants; generation assignments; table transitions; discard and fall-through; explicit requirement *and permission* words; parameterization; relied-on environment assumptions). X1-X8 (rationale; examples; metadata; other protocols; speculation; obligations on non-implementers; unobservable implementation notes; the monitor role). T1-T12 with an explicit precedence order, T5 firing last as the residual.

**Three decisions worth flagging:**

1. Normativity and Stage B's scope disposition are held orthogonal. A byte-order rule is a normative row that sits in the denominator carrying barrier `OUT-WIRE`. T12 (stated first, outranking everything) voids any classification argument that rests on unverifiability.
2. Two reading decisions are recorded rather than assumed: R-1 reads the present indicative as requirement mood (the literal alternative, given lines 199 and 307 calling the algorithm "similar to the following" and "suggested", yields a denominator of zero), and R-2 reads the document as specifying conformance despite the line 45-50 disclaimer, resting on lines 106-110 rather than on the external STD 37 fact.
3. The barrier list is closed now, before results exist: `OUT-WIRE`, `OUT-ORDER`, `OUT-ENVIRONMENT`, `OUT-ROLE`, `OUT-BY-SOURCE`, `OUT-MULTINODE`, `UNDERSPECIFIED`. One code per row, no "not attempted", extension only by logged amendment.

Section 8 states the consequence of the conservative tie-break plainly: T5 and T11 over-include on purpose (the entire aging discussion at 415-457 rows and then leaves under `OUT-BY-SOURCE`), so the exclusion ratio carries no information and will not be reported; the gate measures the nine coverage classes and the closed barrier list instead. Section 9 works eleven hard cases as binding demonstrations, including the two halves of the definition tie-break inside the single paragraph at lines 115-126, and the monitor sentence at 337-340 that conflicts with the target gate at line 214.
